Coastal wetlands, including salt marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as nature’s first line of defense against storm surges and coastal flooding. Their ability to mitigate damage is not merely a matter of standing in the way of water, but rather a complex interaction of hydrology and botany that dissipates energy before it reaches human infrastructure.

